canon_reply: ### When a hash table stops being “fast”\n\n| Cause | What happens | Typical impact |\n|-------|--------------|----------------|\n| **Too many collisions** | Many keys map to the same bucket. | The bucket becomes a list/chain or a probe sequence that must be walked → O(n) in the worst case. |\n| **High load factor** | The table is almost full (e.g., 0.9–1.0). | Each bucket holds more entries → longer chains or more probing → more cache misses and more work per lookup. |\n| **Poor hash function** | The hash distributes keys unevenly (e.g., all keys hash to 0). | Same as collisions: one bucket gets all entries → lookup degrades to linear scan. |\n| **Expensive hash computation** | The key is large or the hash algorithm is complex. | The cost of computing the hash dominates the lookup time. |\n| **Resizing in progress** | The table is being rehashed to a larger array. | Lookups may be blocked or need to check two tables → extra overhead. |\n| **Memory locality loss** | The table is huge or spread across many pages. | Cache misses increase; each lookup may touch many memory locations. |\n| **Concurrent access** | Multiple threads read/write without proper synchronization. | Locks or atomic operations add contention; in lock‑free designs, memory barriers can slow things down. |\n| **Distributed hash tables** | Keys are stored on remote nodes. | Network latency dominates the lookup time. |\n\n---\n\n#### Why these factors matter\n\n1. **Collisions & load factor**  \n   *With chaining*: each bucket is a linked list (or vector). If the average list length is *k*, lookup time is *O(k)*.  \n   *With open addressing*: a long probe sequence means many memory accesses and more cache misses.  \n   Keeping the load factor below a threshold (often 0.7–0.75) keeps *k* small.\n\n2. **Hash function quality**  \n   A good hash spreads keys uniformly across the table. If the hash is deterministic but biased, you’ll see “hot” buckets that become bottlenecks.\n\n3. **Hash cost**  \n   For simple keys (integers, small strings) the hash is cheap. For complex objects (large structs, composite keys) the hash may need to walk the whole object, adding noticeable overhead.\n\n4. **Resizing**  \n   Rehashing copies all entries to a new array. During this time, lookups may need to check both the old and new tables or be blocked, which can spike latency.\n\n5. **Cache behavior**  \n   A hash table is an array, so it’s cache‑friendly when the array fits in L1/L2. If the table grows beyond cache size, each lookup may miss the cache, especially if probing jumps around.\n\n6. **Concurrency**  \n   Locks serialize access; lock‑free designs introduce memory barriers and atomic operations that can slow down otherwise trivial lookups.\n\n7. **Distributed systems**  \n   The “hash” is often used to decide which node holds the key. The network round‑trip dominates the cost, making the hash table look slow even though the local lookup is O(1).\n\n---\n\n#### Practical take‑aways\n\n- **Choose a good hash function** (e.g., MurmurHash, xxHash) and test it on your key distribution.\n- **Keep the load factor low**; many libraries let you set a threshold that triggers resizing.\n- **Avoid expensive key hashing**; if you must hash large objects, cache the hash value.\n- **Monitor collision statistics**; if you see a few buckets with many entries, the hash is bad.\n- **Resize proactively** (before the table gets too full) to avoid long probe sequences.\n- **Consider the memory footprint**; a very large table can hurt cache performance.\n- **For concurrent workloads**, use lock‑free or fine‑grained locking schemes, or accept a small slowdown for simplicity.\n\nWhen these conditions are met, a hash table remains a fast, O(1) average‑case data structure. When they’re violated, lookups can degrade to linear time or suffer from high latency due to cache misses, contention, or network delays.
